[THOUGHT] No recent illness or trauma — makes myocarditis or traumatic brain injury less likely. Training intensity is vague; however, exertional collapse in a young athlete without prodrome remains highly suspicious for an underlying cardiac channelopathy or structural abnormality (e.g., anomalous coronary artery). Since history is unrevealing so far, the next critical step is targeted physical examination (domain #7), especially cardiovascular: auscultation for murmurs (e.g., HCM-related systolic murmur that changes with maneuvers), blood pressure differentials (coarctation), peripheral pulses, signs of Marfan or other connective tissue disorders (e.g., pectus deformity, arm span > height, lens subluxation), and neurologic screening (though prior report was negative).

[TEST] Perform a focused cardiovascular and systemic physical examination:  
- Auscultate heart sounds in all positions, including during Valsalva and standing (to assess for dynamic outflow obstruction);  
- Measure bilateral upper and lower extremity blood pressure;  
- Inspect for stigmata of connective tissue disease (e.g., pectus excavatum, high-arched palate, joint hypermobility, arm span-to-height ratio);  
- Assess carotid upstrokes and femoral pulses for delay or asymmetry;  
- Perform brief neurologic screen (gait, coordination, strength, sensation) to confirm prior report.

